"""Human-authored claim ledger. This program serializes; it does not infer verdicts."""
from pathlib import Path
import json,datetime
P=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
C=[]
def add(n,items,scans,lines,words,statement,status,cl,fate,date,refs,comp=None,confidence='high'):
    C.append(dict(id=f'C-SA06-{n}',items=[f'SA06-{i}' for i in items],cote=14,scans=scans,lines=lines,his_statement=words,statement=statement,status_on_pages=status,computation=({'code':'parts/cote14_late/code/recompute.py','output':'parts/cote14_late/audit/recompute_output.json','checks':comp,'proof':'parts/cote14_late/computation_notes.md'} if comp else {'required':'No numerical computation is required; hypothesis/proof analysis is recorded in the statement and fate, and in parts/cote14_late/computation_notes.md where applicable.'}),**{'class':cl},fate=fate,fate_date=date,present_status='As of 2026-11-19: '+fate,refs=refs,confidence=confidence))

add(200,[200,201],[87,89],'Pro5–37,72–88','Hom(A,B)→H¹(A,B) ; application graphe (pas additive!)','For homomorphisms u:A→B of abelian varieties over a separably closed field, the action on H¹ is additive, whereas u↦[Γu] in the full correspondence ring is not additive. The graph determines u, but neither graph formation nor its total cohomology class preserves addition.','proof sketched','E','This is the standard additive H¹ realization of abelian varieties. On an elliptic curve, Γ_[2] acts on H² by4 while Γ_id+Γ_id acts by2; the H¹ actions agree. Milne, Abelian Varieties, I Thm12.1 and the functoriality of Tate modules give the precise realization.','Classical before the published SGA-era formalism; exact manuscript date undetermined.',['MilneAV2008'],['graph_nonadditivity'])
add(201,[201,288],[89,60,76],'Pro77–88; K14-05:20–37; K14-06:104–124','Pic(A×B)/(Pic A×Pic B)≃Hom(A,B*)','For pointed abelian varieties A,B over an algebraically closed field, normalized line bundles on A×B modulo pullbacks from the factors identify with Hom(A,B∨), by pullback of the normalized Poincaré bundle. The unfinished last sentence asks for an unspecified algebraicity criterion on a mixed H¹ class.','proof sketched','E','The divisor/Hom identification is classical Picard duality and the see-saw principle; Milne I §§8–9 supplies the construction. It gives no arbitrary-base Tate theorem: no Galois-invariance condition is supplied in the last sentence. That unfinished strengthening has no identifiable quantifier set.','Classical Picard/Albanese theory; no first-circulation priority asserted.',['MilneAV2008'],confidence='high')
add(202,[202,298],[87,78],'Pro7–12,25–35; K14-06:204–234','H¹(A)×H¹(A*)→Qℓ(+1|-1)','For an abelian variety A over a separably closed field and ℓ different from the characteristic, the Weil pairing is TℓA×TℓA∨→Zℓ(1), while the corresponding perfect pairing of first cohomology is H¹(A,Zℓ)×H¹(A∨,Zℓ)→Zℓ(−1). A line bundle gives a self-dual homomorphism A→A∨ and an alternating Tate pairing.','open on pages','E','Milne I §13, together with Thm12.1, checks the twists and duality. The manuscript sign remains undecided on ink. Its alternating symbol may refer to the resulting pairing, rather than an antisymmetric homomorphism under the canonical double-dual identification.','Classical dual abelian variety/Weil pairing; assessed2026-11-19.',['MilneAV2008'],confidence='high')
add(203,[203],[92],'Pro130–177','groupe libre de type fini?','For smooth projective X over an algebraically closed field, the group of integral codimension-i cycles modulo numerical equivalence is free of finite rank. This settles only the numerical row of the chart; it does not identify cycles modulo algebraic equivalence with that group.','open on pages','B','Kleiman1968 Thm3.5 proves finite freeness using a Weil cohomology. An independent proof chooses finitely many complementary algebraic classes spanning the cohomological algebraic subspace, and embeds the numerical group by integer intersection numbers into Z^r. Algebraic-equivalence quotients can have a nonzero, even infinite-dimensional Griffiths part.','Published1968; precise earlier circulation not established.',['Kleiman1968','Clemens1983'])
add(204,[203,204],[92],'Pro130–177','Groupe fini?; V.A.?','The chart asks for finite differences between homological/algebraic equivalences and for an abelian variety representing a continuous cycle quotient. It does not specify one uniformly valid intermediate Jacobian for all codimensions and characteristics.','open on pages','C','Numerical finite generation is C-SA06-203. Whole Chow or whole algebraically trivial cycle representability is false in general by Mumford1969; algebraic representatives exist in specific codimensions and are universal only for regular homomorphisms, as in Kahn2021 Thm0.1 for codimension2. The chart’s crossed integral/rational homology arrows are not a theorem.','Mumford communicated1968-11-28/published1969; Kahn preprint2018-12-05, journal2021.',['Mumford1969','Kahn2021Regular','Kleiman1968'])
add(205,[206],[90,77],'Pro95–120; K14-06:131–170','Les morphismes ... isomorphismes lorsque q égale0 ou1','For a sheaf F on a site and the Čech cohomology formed by refinement of covers of S, the comparison to derived H^q(S,F) is an isomorphism for q=0,1 and injective for q=2. If the presheaves U↦H^q(U,F) vanish for0<q<n, it is an isomorphism through n and injective in n+1.','proved there','A','SGA4 V Cor3.4 is the exact published counterpart. The Codex continuation on77 supplies local effacement and Čech H⁰ of the positive cohomology presheaves equal to0, so the earlier note’s alleged missing proof step is present in the combined packet.','Seminar1963–1964; published1972, LNM270.',['SGA4II','Stacks03AV','Stacks01FU'])
add(206,[206,302],[77,86],'K14-06:153–170,494–529','H^q(S,F) est ... celui du faisceau abélien sous-jacent','On a ringed topos, cohomology of an A-module F agrees naturally with the cohomology of its underlying abelian sheaf. The comparison is obtained by universal effaceable delta functors.','proved there','A','SGA4 V Cor3.5 gives the exact result and the same effacement method. The claim does not require the forgetful functor to preserve every injective object.','Seminar1963–1964; published1972.',['SGA4II'])
add(207,[205],[88,79],'Pro44–69; K14-06:244–279','La notion ... n’est pas intrinsèque; Definition3.5','For an abelian sheaf F on a topos E, vanishing of H^q(S,F) for every S∈E and q>0 is equivalent to Čech acyclicity for every epimorphic covering family in E. Testing only objects of one presenting site is a site-relative property.','proved there','A','SGA4 V §§4.1–4.5 distinguishes site acyclicity from flasqueness in the topos; Prop4.5 identifies the intrinsic Čech condition. The statement and both implications continue on Codex79 after Pro88.','Published1972; seminar1963–1964.',['SGA4II'])
add(208,[301],[84],'K14-06:436–468','Proposition3.1','For a sheaf F on a site C, the stated vanishing of higher derived cohomology on the chosen site objects is equivalent to the covering/Čech criterion with the exact family and fibre-product conditions written in Proposition3.1.','proved there','A','SGA4 V Prop4.3 is the matching published criterion. It is a result about the selected site objects; it does not identify site acyclicity for every possible presentation.','Published1972.',['SGA4II'])
add(209,[301],[82],'K14-06:357–385','u:C→C′; cofinal','A continuous site functor u preserves the specified acyclicity property when covering families from its source are cofinal among covers of the target images and the relevant finite fibre products are preserved. The proof compares Čech complexes on those covers.','proof sketched','A','The site-level result is checked against SGA4 V §4 and its functorial Čech construction, and independently follows from cofinality of covers plus equality of the Čech terms. Continuity alone is insufficient.','Published SGA4 formalism1972; no separate first-circulation claim.',['SGA4II'])
add(210,[207],[97],'Pro331–344','B̂ est isomorphe ... à B̂′','Published reconstruction: let A be a topological ring, B an A-algebra with J-preadic topology, and C=B/J a formally smooth discrete A-algebra. Then B is formally smooth iff J/J² is projective and Sym_C(J/J²)→gr_JB is an isomorphism, iff the separated completion of B is a completed symmetric C-algebra on a projective module.','proof sketched','A','EGA IV_1,0_IV Cor19.5.4, printed pp91–94, exactly realizes the proposed three-condition rewrite. The hypotheses and C-algebra section come from the published theorem, while the detached page only names prior statements2.2/3.3.','Published1964.',['EGAIV1'])
add(211,[208],[97],'Pro340–344','A contient un corps','A complete Noetherian local ring A contains a field iff it is a quotient of a formal power-series ring k[[T1,…,Tr]] over a coefficient field. If the residue characteristic is p>0, this is equivalent to pA=0. For a domain it is also equivalent to equality of the characteristics of A and its residue field.','stated without proof','E','Cohen’s structure theorem, reproduced precisely in EGA IV_1,0_IV19.6.2–19.6.3 and19.8.8(i), proves the statement. The p>0 relation and double brackets are missing in the transcription and remain readings to check. The complete local hypothesis is essential.','Cohen1946 predates the draft; EGA publication1964.',['EGAIV1','Stacks0323'])
add(212,[209],[99],'Pro375–387','vide, ou un ensemble principal homogène','Let E→C be a square-zero extension of A-algebras with kernel J and let u:B→C. If an A-algebra lift v:B→E exists, all lifts are v+D for unique D∈Der_A(B,J), where J is a B-module via u.','proved there','A','EGA IV_1,0_IV§18.1 and Stacks Tags02H5,04FJ establish the lifting torsor. The square-zero expansion independently proves both directions. The displayed v1:A→E must have domain B for this statement; no ink alteration is made.','Published EGA1964; standard earlier infinitesimal algebra.',['EGAIV1','Stacks02H5','Stacks04FJ'],['square_zero'])
add(213,[210],[101],'Pro455–461; K14-08:2–16','Pour tout ... système i,j,v_i ... il existe k≥j et ... w_j','For a preadmissible A-algebra B with C=B/J, both formally smooth, the inverse systems of truncations and truncated symmetric algebras admit, after enlarging the index, maps w whose degree0 and degree1 associated-graded maps are transitions and whose composition with a chosen v is the transition map.','stated without proof','A','EGA IV_1,0_IV Lem19.5.6.5, p97, is the exact surviving counterpart of C(a); Lem19.5.6.3 supplies the preceding compatible v maps. The publication provides the missing preadmissibility and system definitions. C(b) is entirely cancelled and must not be promoted to a surviving extra assertion.','Published1964.',['EGAIV1'])
add(214,[211],[104],'Pro513–534; K14-08:58–97','e+codim_Y{y}≥trdeg_{κ(y)}κ(x)+codim_X{x}','For an irreducible finite-type dominant morphism X→Y of locally Noetherian schemes, generic relative transcendence degree e, and x over y, one has e+codim_Y(y)≥trdeg_{κ(y)}κ(x)+codim_X(x). Equality holds under the stated quotient-of-regular-local-ring hypothesis at y, in particular under universal catenarity.','proof sketched','A','EGA IV_2,5.6.5 and Stacks Lemma10.113.1(Tag02II) give the exact dimension formula and equality hypothesis. If x is closed in its finite-type fibre, its residue extension is algebraic, so the displayed special case follows.','Published1965.',['EGAIV2','Stacks02II'])
add(215,[296],[75],'K14-06:46–70','Consequently; F(X,Y)=X+Y+λXY','Over a field, an associative polynomial law on A¹ with two-sided unit0 is either F(x,y)=x+y+axy, with a scalar a; imposing a polynomial inverse makes a=0. The page’s leading-degree argument gives degree1 in each variable.','proved there','E','An independent polynomial-degree proof and the inverse formula −x/(1+ax) verify the statement over a field. The final antipode argument is a completion by this run, not text after the manuscript’s terminal Consequently. This does not claim the same classification over nonreduced base rings.','Classical affine-line group law; no exact first source identified by the bounded SGA3/algebraic-group search.',['MilneAlgebraicGroups2017'],['polynomial_group_law'])
add(216,[216],[94,95],'Pro188–278','Les propriétés suivantes sont équivalentes; a) L est ample','For a proper scheme X over a field, a line bundle L is ample iff χ(Y,L^n|Y) tends to+∞ for every positive-dimensional integral closed subscheme Y; equivalently the corresponding growth holds for every coherent sheaf with positive-dimensional support. The argument uses induction, additivity and eventual cohomology vanishing.','proof sketched','B','Mumford–Oda, Algebraic Geometry II,VIII Thm1.11, printedp288, proves the exact Euler-characteristic criterion. It is stronger and safer than the fixed thresholds in b″/c″ when the constants field is not the base field.','Mumford’s argument appears in Grothendieck–Mumford correspondence1964; modern edition2015.',['MumfordOda2015','GrothMumfordLetters'])
add(217,[217],[94],'Pro199–236','si dim Z=1 χ≥2; h⁰≥2','As literally stated over an arbitrary field, the criterion that some power has χ≥2 (or h⁰≥2) on every integral curve, with threshold1 in higher dimensions, does not imply ampleness.','false as stated','B','Counterexample: X=P¹_C viewed as a projective integral R-scheme, L=O_X. It has dimension1, degree0 and is not ample, but χ_R(O_X)=h⁰_R(O_X)=2 for every power. Replacing the constant2 by a threshold exceeding dim_k H⁰(O_Z), or assuming k algebraically closed, removes this particular obstruction; no silent base-field amendment is made.','Counterexample checked in this run2026-11-19; positive Euler criterion published as in C-SA06-216.',['MumfordOda2015'],['surface_RR'])
add(218,[218],[95],'Pro237–258','Lemme1; H^i(X,L^n)=0 pour i≥2','For projective integral X and a line bundle L whose restrictions to every proper positive-dimensional integral subvariety are ample, the draft proves eventual vanishing H^i(X,L^n)=0 for i≥2 by cutting with a hyperplane and induction.','proof sketched','B','This is the inductive mechanism in the cohomological ampleness criterion of Mumford–Oda VIII Thm1.11. The projectivity annotation is used to choose a hyperplane; replacing projective by arbitrary proper at this intermediate step requires a separate proof, though the final numerical criterion is known for proper X.','1964 correspondence; later exposition2015.',['MumfordOda2015','GrothMumfordLetters'])
add(219,[218],[95],'Pro259–278','Lemme2; H⁰(X,L^n)≠0','Under the same restriction-ampleness hypothesis, dim X≥2, and existence of a nonzero section of some positive power of L, the draft uses its effective divisor and induction to obtain eventual global generation and separation from a constant map, hence ampleness.','proof sketched','B','The effective-divisor step is part of the Mumford–Oda ampleness proof. A nonzero section with empty divisor gives the trivial bundle, incompatible with positive restriction on a curve in a positive-dimensional projective X; a nonempty divisor enables induction. The manuscript asks whether the section hypothesis can be removed.','1964 correspondence; exposition2015.',['MumfordOda2015','GrothMumfordLetters'])
add(220,[218,219],[95,96],'Pro271–329','Peut-on se passer ... H⁰; D.C>0','The hoped-for removal of the nonzero-section condition from the surface restriction-ampleness test would assert that strict positivity on every curve suffices for ampleness.','open on pages','B','It is false over C: Hartshorne1970,I Example10.6,p56, gives Mumford’s smooth projective ruled surface with a divisor D satisfying D.C>0 for every curve but D²=0. All proper positive-dimensional subvarieties are curves and D restricts amply to each, while D is not ample.','Counterexample attributed by Hartshorne to Mumford via Kleiman1966p326; explicit1970publicationchecked.',['Hartshorne1970','Kleiman1966'])
add(221,[219,221],[96],'Pro279–329','R=int(P^+); peut-on ... D²=0','The nef cone is the closure of the ample cone in the finite-dimensional real numerical divisor space of a projective variety; strict positivity on each individual curve is weaker than belonging to the interior of the dual cone. A strictly nef surface divisor may have square0.','open on pages','B','Kleiman’s numerical criterion and Mumford’s counterexample distinguish these conditions. Hartshorne I Ex10.6,p56 checks the square-zero example; Mumford–Oda IX Thm1.15 gives the cone criterion. The draft’s strict-curve question is thus settled negatively, while the closed-cone equality holds.','Kleiman1966; Hartshorne1970.',['Kleiman1966','Hartshorne1970','MumfordOda2015'])
add(222,[220],[94,98,100],'Pro190–197,346–365,389–446','D^d>0; D.C>0','On a smooth projective threefold, positivity on every curve together with D³>0 does not suffice for ampleness. Intermediate-dimensional subvarieties also matter.','stated without proof','B','Independently construct X=P_S(O⊕A) over Mumford’s surface S, with A ample and L strictly nef with L²=0. For D=ξ+π*L, D is strictly nef, D³=A²+3A.L>0, but its square on the quotient section O is L²=0; hence it is not ample. Hartshorne I Ex10.8,pp57–58 gives Ramanujam’s related effective threefold counterexample.','Underlying surface example known by1966; Ramanujam example published1970; displayed variant checked2026.',['Hartshorne1970','MumfordOda2015'],['strict_nef_threefold'])
add(223,[222],[100],'Pro389–408','les puissances de D ... positives','For a nef divisor D on a smooth projective variety, powers D^r are limits of classes of complete intersections of ample rational divisors and lie in the pseudoeffective cone of codimension r; their intersection with every effective complementary cycle is nonnegative.','proof sketched','B','Approximate D by D+εH with H ample and pass to the limit in the finite-dimensional numerical cycle space. This elementary divisor argument does not extend to arbitrary higher-codimension nef classes. DELV2011 §1 reviews the distinction and TheoremA supplies counterexamples to that extension.','Divisor theory established by Kleiman1966; higher-codimension contrast2010preprint/2011publication.',['Kleiman1966','DELV2011'])
add(224,[223],[98,100],'Pro346–365,389–446','Est-ce que le produit de deux cycles numériquement positifs ... positif','For nef classes of arbitrary codimension, neither pseudoeffectivity nor closure under products follows merely from nonnegative intersection with effective complementary cycles.','open on pages','B','Debarre–Ein–Lazarsfeld–Voisin2011,TheoremA and Remark2.3, explicitly connect the question to Grothendieck’s31Aug1964letter and show on powers of a CM elliptic curve that nef higher-codimension classes can fail to be pseudoeffective and products of nef classes can fail to be nef. The precise numerical positivity cone is essential.','arXiv1003.3183March2010; published2011.',['DELV2011','GrothMumfordLetters'])
add(225,[223],[98],'Pro346–365','α_{d−1}(P_{d−1}^{+0})⊂P_1^+','The diagram asks whether a duality map α_i sends the interior of a dual positivity cone to a positive cone. The notation α_i, the equivalence relation and even the written A^{2d−i} grading are not completely defined on this sheet.','open on pages','F','The 1964 letter and DELV2011 identify nearby higher-cycle cone questions, but do not fix the exact α_i used here. The A^{2d−i} entry is dimensionally incompatible with the likely codimension convention and is retained as a reading alarm. No distinct unresolved theorem is inferred from this fragment.','Comparison with letter1964; bounded identification search through2026-11-19.',['GrothMumfordLetters','DELV2011'],confidence='medium')
add(226,[224],[96],'Pro304–329','χ(L^n)=χ(O_X)+nχ(D)','For a smooth projective surface and divisor D with D²=0, Riemann–Roch gives χ(O_X(nD))=χ(O_X)−n(K_X.D)/2. The symbol χ(D) on the sheet is the linear coefficient, not χ(O_X(D)).','proved there','E','The calculation is exact. If D is strictly nef and not numerically trivial, no positive multiple can be represented by a nonzero effective divisor when D²=0. Together with eventual H²-vanishing this bounds the linear coefficient by0. Whether the written sign is < or≤ remains an ink question; the argument alone does not decide the glyph.','Surface Riemann–Roch predates manuscript; independent recalculation2026.',['Hartshorne1970','MumfordOda2015'],['surface_RR'])
add(227,[220,222,223],[100],'Pro412–446','(nD+D′)^3','The cubic expands as n³D³+3n²D²D′+3nD(D′)²+(D′)³; sign arguments about the coefficients require the surrounding cone and intersection hypotheses, not merely this identity.','proved there','E','The symbolic expansion is correct. It yields no general positivity theorem for products of higher-codimension nef cycles, which DELV2011 refutes.','Elementary identity; recalculated2026-11-19.',['DELV2011'],['cubic_expansion'])

add(228,[225],[103],'Pro475–505; K14-08:26–57','noyau K tel que K∩J¹(X) fini, donc K de type fini','For a smooth projective surface X and smooth hyperplane curve Y over an algebraically closed field, restriction on NS has finite cokernel and kernel of rankρ(X)−1. Restriction Pic⁰(X)→Pic⁰(Y) has finite kernel, with the prime-to-characteristic injection expressed by the page. Consequently ker(Pic(X)→Pic(Y)) is finitely generated.','proof sketched','B','Finite generation follows because its intersection with Pic⁰ is finite and its image in the finitely generated NS(X) is a subgroup. The numerical map need not have finite kernel: the page says finite cokernel and rankρ−1 kernel. The Picard/Albanese hyperplane comparison is classical and is compatible with SGA2 XII and Milne III §10.','SGA2 seminar1962/published1968; independent group-theoretic implication checked2026.',['SGA2','MilneAV2008','Kleiman1968'])
add(229,[226],[103],'Pro501–505','En général ... K fini; Je présume ... courbes irréductibles','The draft expects the Picard restriction kernel to be finite for a sufficiently general smooth hyperplane curve, and suggests irreducibility of limiting fibres in a pencil as a sufficient condition. No exact meaning of general or precise pencil hypotheses is fixed.','open on pages','C','For a regular surface pencil with every fibre geometrically integral, a line bundle trivial on the generic fibre is vertical and, after imposing the base-point exceptional-section data, one can often force the original surface bundle to be trivial. This verifies the generic-fibre mechanism, not the stated special-fibre assertion for an arbitrary chosen Y. The MathOverflow233157 argument gives precise very-general hypersurface cases and points back to Lefschetz-pencil theory; no theorem with exactly the draft’s unquantified formulation was identified.','Modern comparison2016; bounded search through2026-11-19.',['SGA2','Starr2016Picard'],confidence='medium')
add(230,[227],[106,108,109],'Pro539–583,624–688; K14-08:98–143,190–264','N^i(X)≡0; J^i(X)≡0; A^i(X)≡0','With S=Spec A strictly local Noetherian, X=S minus the closed point, f in the maximal ideal, Y=X∩V(f), and X,Y regular, the pages conjecture high-codimension finiteness/vanishing for N,J,A and parity-dependent critical pieces. N is inferred to mean dim A; N^i and J^i require the intended local cycle equivalence definition.','open on pages','C','The literal all-degree package is not a standard established theorem. Codimension1 local Lefschetz has precise modern results under additional normality, excellence, dimension and depth hypotheses (Bhatt–de Jong Thm0.1); complete-intersection local rings of dimension≥4 are parafactorial (Stacks0F2I). Neither source proves higher-Chow finiteness for arbitrary strictly local Noetherian A. The undefined local equivalence convention limits exact identification of N and J.','SGA2published1968; Bhatt–de Jong2014; assessed2026-11-19.',['SGA2','BhattDeJong2014','Stacks0F2I'],confidence='medium')
add(231,[227],[106,107],'Pro559–602; K14-08:118–177','J^i(X)→J^i(Y); N^i(X)→N^i(Y); A^i(X)→A^i(Y)','The local restriction conjectures give J-isomorphisms for i≤(N−2)/2 and injections for i≤(N−1)/2; N-isomorphisms for i≤(N−3)/2 and injections for i≤(N−2)/2, with prime-characteristic qualifications. The displayed whole-A range is the lower N range.','open on pages','C','Bhatt–de Jong2014 Thm0.1 proves injectivity of local Picard restriction for excellent normal local A containing a field, dim A≥4; in characteristic0 it requires depth(A/f)≥2, while in characteristic p its kernel is p-power torsion. Their examples show the dimension/depth restrictions matter and surjectivity is not automatic. This is a restricted codimension1 realization, not the complete integral Chow package.','Published2014, pp833–849.',['BhattDeJong2014','SGA2'],confidence='medium')
add(232,[228],[107],'Pro589–605; K14-08:152–177','A^i(X)→A^i(U) un isom mod grp finis; A^i(Y)→A^{i+1}(X) nul','For the local principal divisor Y⊂X and U=X−Y, the draft predicts that Gysin pushforward CH^i(Y)→CH^{i+1}(X) vanishes (up to finite groups in the specified ranges), equivalently that restriction to U is an isomorphism in those ranges.','open on pages','C','Chow localization makes the stated implication exact once Gysin vanishing is supplied. The fact that Y is principal only says its divisor class acts trivially on classes pulled back from X; it does not by itself force pushforward of every cycle on Y to vanish. The general vanishing hypothesis is not established by local Picard Lefschetz.','Localization formalism classical/Fulton1984; local Picard advances2014.',['Stacks02RX','Fulton1998','BhattDeJong2014'])
add(233,[229,264],[107],'Pro607–611; K14-08:177–189','Définir des formes ... Z??; une théorie des cycles évanescents','Construct local intersection pairings on the critical cycle groups and a vanishing-cycle theory compatible with the local restriction maps. The page asks for the objects and forms; it supplies no singular correspondence category.','open on pages','C','Mumford1961 §1 proves negative definiteness of exceptional-curve intersections for a normal complex surface singularity. Deligne1980 Thm4.1.1 and§4.1 give cohomological Lefschetz and invariant cycles. These do not define the manuscript’s proposed integral higher-Chow pairings or its J-valued vanishing-cycle group.','Mumford1961; Deligne1980; higher-cycle formulation still unspecified2026.',['Mumford1961','Deligne1980WeilII'],confidence='medium')
add(234,[229],[107],'Pro612–622; K14-08:180–189','N=2; N¹(X) fini: c’est le th. de Mumford-Hodge','In dimension2 the page identifies finiteness of the local discrete divisor group with a Mumford–Hodge theorem. The standard precise model is the finite discriminant group of the exceptional intersection lattice on a resolution of an isolated normal complex surface singularity.','stated without proof','B','Mumford1961 §1,p6 proves the intersection matrix is negative definite; its nonzero integral determinant gives a finite lattice cokernel. Identification with the particular locally defined N¹ on this sheet requires its omitted definition and the resolution hypotheses. The finite discriminant group must not be confused with the full local Picard group.','Published1961.',['Mumford1961'],confidence='medium')
add(235,[230],[108,109],'Pro645–678; K14-08:211–250','définir un complémentaire naturel; J¹(X)≃J¹(Y)^π','The page asks for natural complements to the old Picard or discrete cycle classes in a hyperplane section and an interpretation as monodromy invariants, in the two critical parities.','open on pages','C','Cohomological invariant-cycle theorems identify invariant subspaces under precise smooth proper pencil hypotheses. They do not produce canonical complements of abelian varieties integrally or of Chow groups; Poincaré complete reducibility yields complements only up to isogeny and after choices of polarization. The stronger local cycle statement remains a programme.','Classical Poincaré reducibility; Deligne1980§4.1.',['Deligne1980WeilII','MilneAV2008'])
add(236,[231],[108],'Pro655–665; K14-08:225–237','J¹ bij; N¹ inj; ⇒A¹ bij','From a morphism of short exact sequences0→J→A→N→0, an isomorphism on J and an injection on N imply injectivity on A, not surjectivity. The displayed implication to bijectivity lacks a right-hand surjectivity input.','false as stated','E','The split rational countermodel J=J′=Q, A=Q, A′=Q², N=0,N′=Q, with the evident maps, satisfies the two premises but not the conclusion. It survives every finite-error or prime-to-p qualification. This refutes the formal inference, not a separately proved geometric Picard theorem.','Elementary exact-sequence argument; checked2026-11-19.',['BhattDeJong2014'],['five_lemma_countermodel'])
add(237,[232],[111],'Pro690–727; K14-08:265–302','A_r^i(X)→A_r^i(Y) bij si2i≤n−2; inj si2i=n−1','For smooth projective connected X of dimension n over an algebraically closed field and a smooth ample hyperplane Y, the page conjectures integral Chow restriction isomorphism for2i≤n−2, injection at2i=n−1, and additional algebraic/τ refinements with characteristic exceptions.','open on pages','C','The rational isomorphism in codimension i<dimY/2 is the weak Lefschetz conjecture for Chow groups. Patel–Ravindra2014 Conj1.1 states it; Thm1.4 proves an infinitesimal K-cohomological result, not algebraization. In codimension1 classical Grothendieck–Lefschetz applies under its dimension/depth conditions. No universal integral finite-error theorem in the written range was found.','SGA2seminar1962/published1968; Patel–Ravindra2014; bounded updated search2026.',['PatelRavindra2014','SGA2'])
add(238,[232,234],[111,112],'Pro728–749; K14-08:303–322','A_i^r(Y)→A_i^r(X) surjectif si2i=n−2','The proposed Gysin map on dimension-i Chow groups is an isomorphism for2i≤n−3 and surjective at2i=n−2, with extra assertions on algebraically or τ-trivial parts.','false as stated','B','At n=2,i=0, take a smooth projective complex K3 surface X and any smooth hyperplane curve Y. Surjectivity CH0(Y)_Q→CH0(X)_Q would make CH0(X) finite-dimensional in Mumford’s sense, contrary to H⁰(X,Ω²)≠0 and Mumford1969. The same obstruction applies to the surjectivity on the degree-zero/algebraically trivial part. The quotient modulo algebraic equivalence is a different, much smaller assertion.','Refutation follows from Mumford1969, communicated1968.',['Mumford1969','BeauvilleVoisin2004'])
add(239,[233,239],[112,117],'Pro751–757,928–950; K14-08:323–331','A_a^i(X)→A_a^{n−i}(X) bijectif mod groupes finis','The manuscript uses A_a=cycles modulo algebraic equivalence, and conjectures L^{n−2i}:A_a^i→A_a^{n−i} is bijective up to finite groups for2i≤n. It is not the same assertion with numerical or homological equivalence.','false as stated','B','For a very general smooth quintic threefold over C and i=1, NS(X)_Q is finite-dimensional, while CH²(X)/alg tensored with Q contains an infinite-dimensional Griffiths group: Clemens1983 Thm0.2 plus Griffiths’s vanishing of the algebraic Abel–Jacobi image, stated there. Hence L cannot be surjective even rationally. The standard Lefschetz conjecture on cohomological algebraic classes remains a distinct question.','Griffiths1968–1969 obstructions; stronger Clemens result1983.',['Clemens1983','Kleiman1968'])
add(240,[233,239],[112,117],'Pro758–767,928–950; K14-08:332–347','A_r^i(X)^τ→A_r^{n−i+1}(X)^τ; E^{n−2i+1}','The draft conjectures a shifted hard Lefschetz isomorphism up to finite groups on the τ-trivial subgroup of the Chow group. Later notation distinguishes algebraically trivial J from the discrete algebraic-equivalence quotient.','false as stated','B','In the n=2,i=1 case, a complex K3 has Pic⁰=0 and nonzero CH0^0, so the analogous algebraically trivial map cannot be surjective. With τ interpreted as numerical or homological triviality up to torsion, Pic^τ of a K3 is also0, and the same obstruction applies. The explicit change of subscript on the page must be retained; no conjecture on a motivic associated-graded piece is written.','Mumford1969 supplies the counterexample mechanism.',['Mumford1969','BeauvilleVoisin2004'])
add(241,[234],[112],'Pro762–767; K14-08:339–347','A_r^i(X)→A_r^{i+p}(X); surjectif si p≥n−2i+1','The claimed consequence asserts whole-Chow multiplication by L^p is injective up to finite groups for p≤n−2i and surjective for p≥n−2i+1.','false as stated','B','For n=2,i=1,p=1 on a complex projective K3, Beauville–Voisin2004 Thm1 places all divisor products in Q·c_X, whereas Mumford1969 shows CH0(X)_Q is not finite-dimensional. Thus even the rational surjectivity fails. Laterveer2016 discusses refined injectivity on Abel–Jacobi-trivial cycles and proves conditional results, not this universal whole-Chow statement.','Mumford1969; Beauville–Voisin2004; Laterveer first2015/published2016.',['Mumford1969','BeauvilleVoisin2004','Laterveer2016'],['K3_surjectivity_test'])
add(242,[235],[113],'Pro771–781; K14-08:348–364','A_r^i(U)≃A_r^i(X)/Im A_r^i(Y)','For a smooth divisor Y⊂X and U=X−Y, Chow localization is CH^{i−1}(Y)→CH^i(X)→CH^i(U)→0. The page’s same-codimension source is incompatible with a codimension1 closed immersion. The asserted vanishing on U depends on its Gysin conjecture.','false as stated','E','Stacks Tag02RX (localization) and Fulton §1.8 give the correct degree shift. The correction repairs the exact sequence, not the false general Chow Gysin-surjectivity claim C-SA06-238.','Classical rational-equivalence localization; published Fulton1984/1998.',['Stacks02RX','Fulton1998'],['localization_degree'])
add(243,[235,236],[113],'Pro783–816; K14-08:365–401','cône projectant affine épointé ... dim2n+1; partie primitive','The punctured affine cone of an n-dimensional smooth projective X is a G_m-bundle of dimension n+1, with CH^i equal to the quotient of CH^i(X) by multiplication by the hyperplane class from CH^{i−1}(X). The draft links this to high-codimension finiteness and primitive classes.','false as stated','C','The dimension2n+1 and primitive-kernel exponent n−2i are incompatible with the standard cone and Lefschetz conventions: the usual primitive kernel on H^{2i} uses L^{n−2i+1}. Localization gives the quotient unconditionally; identifying it with a primitive part needs a proven Lefschetz decomposition in the chosen equivalence theory. The manuscript’s whole-Chow finiteness conclusions do not follow.','Classical bundle localization and Lefschetz theory; modern Chow refinements partly developed.',['Stacks02RX','Fulton1998','Kleiman1968','Laterveer2016'],['localization_degree','primitive_exponents'])
add(244,[237],[115,116],'Pro838–907; K14-08:419–504','cas n=2; n=3; OK si équiv rationnelle=équiv Albanese','The low-dimensional table checks consequences of the global conjectures conditionally on identifying rational equivalence of zero-cycles with Albanese equivalence. The handwritten OK marks explicitly depend on that hypothesis.','open on pages','B','Roitman’s theorem controls torsion, not the whole Albanese kernel: Milne1982 Thm0.1 gives the full torsion isomorphism over algebraically closed fields for smooth projective X. Mumford1969 refutes the full rational/Albanese equivalence already for surfaces with nonzero2forms. Thus the table is not a proof in dimensions2 or3.','Roitman1980; prime-characteristic completionMilne1982; obstructionMumford1969.',['Milne1982Roitman','Mumford1969'])
add(245,[238],[116],'Pro908–926; K14-08:505–527','cas n=4; ?; partie intime|entière','The dimension4 table isolates unanswered maps between Chow, algebraic-equivalence and continuous parts, with several question marks and an uncertain adjective.','open on pages','C','The degree-specific cohomological Lefschetz theorem is established, but none of the checked primary results supplies the full integral cycle table. For abelian varieties, Künnemann1993 Thm5.2 gives rational Chow-motivic Lefschetz and Beauville2008 Prop5.5 gives explicit Beauville-graded Chow maps. These are restricted settings and shifted gradings, not solutions for arbitrary fourfolds.','Künnemann1993; Beauville2008; assessed2026-11-19.',['Kunnemann1993','Beauville2008','Laterveer2016'],confidence='medium')
add(246,[239],[117],'Pro928–950','Il ... semble pas ... partie primitive raisonnable pour A^*(X)','The manuscript separates primitive cohomology P, a putative primitive algebraic-equivalence part M, and the algebraically trivial subgroup I, and questions whether the whole Chow group admits a reasonable primitive part.','open on pages','C','Rational Chow motives of abelian schemes have a Lefschetz decomposition (Künnemann1993), but ordinary Chow groups require extra Beauville gradings and shifts (Beauville2008 Prop5.5). The draft does not define a universal decomposition of the full Chow ring, and the failed surjectivity examples explain the limitation.','1993 and2008 results; general programme remains qualified2026.',['Kunnemann1993','Beauville2008','Mumford1969'])
add(247,[241],[118],'Pro952–970','cycles numériquement équivalents à0 ... famille limitée','The page conjectures that numerically trivial cycles modulo rational equivalence admit representatives in a bounded finite-dimensional algebraic family, and expects the quotient to be an abelian variety. In codimension dimX this concerns degree-zero zero-cycles.','false as stated','B','Mumford1969,pp195–196 and its theorem, proves that for a smooth projective complex surface with H⁰(Ω²)≠0, zero-cycles are not finite-dimensional in this sense. A product of two elliptic curves is a concrete smooth projective example; its degree-zero cycles are numerically trivial. Grothendieck’s20Nov1968letter to Mumford explicitly recognizes failure of the rectangle claim on such a product.','Mumford communicated1968-11-28/published1969; Grothendieck letter1968-11-20.',['Mumford1969','GrothMumfordLetters'])
add(248,[242,243],[118,126],'Pro968–978,1091–1127','(a−a′)×(b−b′)∼_a0','For connected smooth projective varieties A,B, the rectangle (a−a′)×(b−b′) is algebraically equivalent to0. Replacing ∼_a by rational equivalence is a strictly stronger assertion and is false in general.','false as stated','B','The written algebraic-equivalence assertion is automatic by varying one point in a connected family; it cannot imply CH0=Alb. The rational-equivalence strengthening fails on a product of complex elliptic curves by Mumford’s theorem and is explicitly rejected in the1968letter. Therefore the claimed consequences rest on an equivalence-relation mismatch, not a false algebraic-equivalence fact.','Grothendieck letter1968-11-20; Mumford1969.',['Mumford1969','GrothMumfordLetters'])
add(249,[244,245,246],[126,128],'Pro1091–1127,1149–1225','δ_A:A(k)→A_0(A); est un homomorphisme; est un isom','For an abelian variety A with origin0, the rational rectangle identity implies additivity of δ_A(a)=[a]−[0] in CH0(A)^0. If δ_A is additive, it is inverse to the Albanese map. Conversely Alb injectivity forces all rectangle products to vanish after pushforward from products.','proof sketched','B','The equivalences under the explicitly rational rectangle hypothesis are elementary, but their universal premise is false in dimension≥2 (Mumford1969). Kahn2021 highlights the related pitfall that the cycle-family map B(k)→CH²_alg can be only a set map although its composite with a regular homomorphism is a group homomorphism. Universal regular quotients do not identify the full cycle group.','Refutation1969; Kahn2018letter/2021journal.',['Mumford1969','Kahn2021Regular'])
add(250,[247],[126,128],'Pro1091–1127,1149–1225','Albanese ... isomorphisme','The unconditional comparison that survives for smooth projective X over an algebraically closed field is that the Albanese map induces an isomorphism on torsion zero-cycles; it is not generally injective on all degree-zero cycles.','open on pages','B','Milne1982 Thm0.1 extends Roitman’s prime-to-characteristic torsion theorem to the p-primary part. This is a later restricted answer, not a claim stated with these torsion hypotheses on the sheets. Mumford’s nontrivial Albanese kernel remains compatible with the torsion theorem.','Roitman1980; Milne1982.',['Milne1982Roitman','Mumford1969'])

add(251,[248],[120],'Pro979–1009','Conditions équivalentes; ξ+γ(O(−n)^N)=γ(F+R)','For a projective scheme X, a set M⊂K0(X) is represented by differences of two bounded families of vector bundles iff there are common integers n,N and finitely many Hilbert polynomials such that the representatives are locally free quotients of O_X(−n)^N. A fixed shift then represents M by one bounded quotient family.','proof sketched','A','Grothendieck1961,Techniques de construction IV,Thm2.1 gives the boundedness criterion; Prop1.2(i) gives bounded kernels of maps in bounded families. In the written calculation R=ker(O(−n)^N→G) is locally free and ξ+[O(−n)^N]=[F⊕R]. Boundedness supplies a second common twist and number of generators. The stopped sentence is therefore completed by a published theorem, not a claim that all kernels have the same original twist.','BourbakiExposé221May1961; collected seminar publication.',['GrothHilbert1961'])
add(252,[249],[121,123],'Pro1010–1025,1047–1054','(ii)⇒(i)⇔(iv)⇒(iii)⇒(v); (i−1)!ζ=c^i(ξ)','For smooth projective X the five cycle-boundedness conditions on121 are connected by the partial implication chain on123. The two sheets belong together despite an intervening reused Cartan leaf. They compare bounded cycle representatives, bounded classes in a coniveau filtration of K0, a factorial Chern-class relation, and numerical boundedness.','proof sketched','C','Grothendieck1961 controls bounded sheaf families; Stacks0FDQ and0FEW give the Chow-to-associated-graded K0 map and rational isomorphism. The leading Chern coefficient is (−1)^{i−1}(i−1)!; an overall sign is absorbable by changing the K0 representative but must be stated. The precise manuscript K-equivalence is not defined, so the full integral equivalence cannot be recovered from these sources. The deleted heading Conditions équivalentes is not restored.','Boundedness1961; modern Chow/K0 comparison; exact integral draft partly identifiable.',['GrothHilbert1961','Stacks0FDQ','Stacks0FEW','Fulton1998'],['factorial_K_to_Chow'],confidence='medium')
add(253,[250],[128],'Pro1160–1176','quotient faisceautique ... pas forcément quotient des points','A quotient sheaf G/H need not have (G/H)(k)=G(k)/H(k), because a k-point of the quotient can require a nontrivial H-torsor to lift.','proof sketched','E','Independent example: over R, the fppf quotient G_m/μ2 is G_m via squaring. Its real points include negative numbers, while R*/μ2(R) maps only to positive real numbers. Quotient representability and surjectivity as a sheaf therefore do not settle surjectivity on k-points. Milne Algebraic Groups §5 gives the quotient/sheaf formalism.','Classical descent; example checked2026.',['MilneAlgebraicGroups2017'])
add(254,[251],[131],'Pro1241–1285','A(Y(y))=Im A(X)+Im A(C); Or u_y=u','For a pencil resolved by a blow-up X′→X with smooth centre C and exceptional divisor P(N)≅C×S, the generic-fibre Chow group is generated by restrictions from X together with exceptional contributions from C, provided the base S is rational and Chow classes on C_{k(S)} descend from C.','proof sketched','B','The blow-up and projective-bundle formulas give the displayed decomposition; localization over the generic point and homotopy invariance give surjectivity CH(C)→CH(C_{k(t)}) for a rational pencil. The notation u_y=u abbreviates this descent, not literal equality of cycles. For an arbitrary base or arbitrary algebraic-equivalence theory, the written argument needs extra hypotheses. Stacks0FVA gives the regular-immersion blow-up identity; Fulton §6.7 is the standard broader formula.','Classical blow-up formula; published Fulton1984/1998.',['Stacks0FVA','Stacks02RX','Fulton1998'])
add(255,[212],[114],'Pro818–836; K14-08:402–418','cd U≤dim A','The entirely cancelled paragraph attempts to bound étale cohomological dimension of U=Spec A[1/f] for a complete local ring by dim A, via a power-series normalization/algebraization argument. The coefficient and residue-field hypotheses are absent.','corrected by himself later','F','With only the visible literal assumptions it is false: A=R[[t]],f=t has dimension1, while R((t)) is formally real and has infinite2-cohomological dimension. A real closure gives a subgroup C2 in its absolute Galois group; H^q(C2,F2)=F2 for everyq. Strictly local residue fields and torsion prime to the characteristic lead to different valid bounds; the deleted draft cannot be identified with one without adding hypotheses.','Whole paragraph withdrawn on the sheet; SGA affine/local cohomology is only a contextual comparison.',['SGA2','SGA4II'],['C2_cohomology_mod2'])
add(256,[213],[124,122],'Pro1055–1088,1026–1046','M=G×^N W; M_v','For an action of a group scheme G on V and an N-stable subscheme W, where N is its normalizer and the flat quotient exists, the incidence scheme is the contracted product G×^N W; its fibre over v describes translates of W containing v.','proof sketched','A','SGA3 XIII §1 constructs the incidence/transporter objects with representability and flatness hypotheses. The typescript pages are cancelled but correspond to reused foundational material, not a new cycle conjecture. The order is124(p2)→122(p3).','SGA3seminar1962–1964/published1970.',['SGA3XIII'])
add(257,[213],[122,124],'Pro1028–1045,1057–1088','T_{M_v}; n⊂m_a','The tangent-space computation identifies infinitesimal transporters through the action derivative and the tangent subspace of W. The dimension inequalities alone do not imply smoothness of the transporter fibre.','proof sketched','A','SGA3 XIII Lem1.1 gives the tangent calculation in its complete setup. To infer smoothness one also needs the dimension equality/regularity hypotheses of the relevant incidence argument; the handwritten question marks on these cancelled leaves do not pinpoint a unique false tangent equation.','Published1970.',['SGA3XIII'])
add(258,[214,270],[129,127],'Pro1226–1237,1130–1138','section régulière; sous-groupe de Cartan ... unique','Let G→S be smooth, separated, of finite type with connected fibres, and suppose its Cartan-subgroup functor is represented by a smooth S-scheme. The regular locus U is open, and the universal Cartan incidence map is an isomorphism above U. Hence each regular section belongs to a unique Cartan subgroup.','proof sketched','A','SGA3 XIII Thm3.1 and Cor3.2,pp180–182 in the re-edition, match the page exactly. These hypotheses are supplied by the publication; in particular the algebraic-group normalizer here has nothing to do with anabelian procyclic normalizers.','Published1970; seminar1962–1964.',['SGA3XIII'])
add(259,[214],[127],'Pro1135–1147','k(T) est une extension unirationnelle de k','Every algebraic torus T over a field k is unirational. If L/k is a finite separable splitting field, Res_{L/k}(T_L) is rational and its norm to T is dominant, since composing with the diagonal T→Res_{L/k}T_L gives multiplication by[L:k].','proved there','E','SGA3 XIII Lem3.4,p182 gives the same proof and attributes the result to Chevalley. Cor3.3 applies it to the generic Cartan fibration of an affine group with toric Cartan subgroups. The manuscript margin restores a cancellation explicitly marked erroneous. Dominance does not require the norm differential to be surjective when the characteristic divides[L:k].','Known before SGA3; published1970.',['SGA3XIII'],['torus_norm'])
add(260,[215],[132],'Pro1286–1312','J; A; B; ...','The detached ring-theoretic fragments and diagrams on132 lack enough definitions or a complete assertion to identify a unique theorem.','stated without proof','F','Full source reading and exact searches against the EGA formal-smoothness material locate97/99/101 but do not identify132. It remains a fragment, not evidence of novelty and not a reconstructed theorem.','Bounded identification search2026-11-19.',['EGAIV1'],confidence='low')
add(261,[288],[59,60,61],'K14-05:3–51','Z de dimension1 sur X×Y; B_X→A_Y','For smooth projective X,Y of dimensions n,m over an algebraically closed field, a one-cycle Z on X×Y induces a homomorphism Pic⁰(X)_red→Alb(Y) by restricting line bundles to the normalization of each curve component, then pushing the resulting degree-zero divisor to Y. It is additive in Z and compatible with rational equivalence.','proof sketched','E','Picard/Albanese functoriality supplies the construction. Numerical invariance of the resulting homomorphism is checked through its mixed cohomology class and the injectivity of Tate realization of homomorphisms (Milne I Thm10.15), together with divisor/one-cycle duality. The statement concerns this realized homomorphism, not equality of full Chow classes.','Classical correspondence theory; no exact first-publication priority assigned.',['MilneAV2008','Kleiman1968'])
add(262,[288,289],[62],'K14-05:52–73','Prop2.3; variante Pic⁰; signes','The draft’s Proposition2.3 compares transposed divisor/curve correspondences with dual abelian homomorphisms, and proposes a variant using the Picard scheme. Its shorthand AQT and one sign convention are not defined in this packet.','open on pages','C','For smooth proper varieties, the reduced Picard identity component and Albanese duality give the standard formulation. Nonreduced Picard schemes in positive characteristic require a group-scheme statement, not merely k-points. Milne I §§8–9,13 verifies the abelian duality; the unknown shorthand and sign remain readings, so no broader representability theorem is attributed.','Classical duality; exact variant incompletely identifiable.',['MilneAV2008'],confidence='medium')
add(263,[289],[62],'K14-05:63–73','isomorphisme pour un l ... tout l; isogénie','For a homomorphism u:A→B of abelian varieties of equal dimension, the induced map on rational ℓ-adic Tate modules (equivalently H¹) is an isomorphism for one ℓ≠char k iff u is an isogeny, hence iff it is an isomorphism for every such ℓ.','proof sketched','E','Milne I §7 and Thm10.15 establish the isogeny/Tate-module criterion. The kernel’s connected finite part can be invisible on prime-to-p torsion, but positive-dimensional kernel cannot; dimensions and finite surjectivity give the stated equivalence.','Classical abelian variety theory.',['MilneAV2008'])
add(264,[289],[63],'K14-05:77–84','inverse ... diviseur rationnel; entier si isomorphisme','An isogeny u:A→B has a unique inverse in Hom(B,A)⊗Q. Via the normalized Poincaré correspondence it is represented by a rational divisor class; it is integral in the Hom lattice iff u is an isomorphism.','proved there','E','The quasi-inverse isogeny relation vu=[N] gives u^{-1}=v/N. If the inverse lies in Hom(B,A), the equalities hold as actual maps since Hom is torsion-free, so u is an isomorphism. Milne I Thm10.15 and the duality construction verify the lattice and functoriality.','Classical isogeny category.',['MilneAV2008'])
add(265,[289],[64],'K14-05:88–90','meilleur dénominateur ... nombre des éléments de Ker','The least positive integer d for which d·u^{-1} is an actual homomorphism is the least d annihilating the finite kernel group scheme, not generally its order.','false as stated','E','For [m] on an elliptic curve over C, the kernel has orderm² but the inverse has minimal denominatorm. More generally [m] on dimensiong has kernel orderm^{2g}. The order supplies an annihilating bound; equality with the best denominator is false. For any isogeny, d·u^{-1} exists exactly when[d] factors through u, equivalently when[d] kills keru as a group scheme.','Counterexample classical; degree formula Milne I Thm7.2.',['MilneAV2008'],['isogeny_denominator'])
add(266,[289],[65],'K14-05:94–110','l premier au dénominateur; isom sur Z_l','An isogeny induces an isomorphism on integral ℓ-adic Tate modules iff its kernel has no ℓ-primary part, equivalently iff ℓ does not divide its degree. The same prime-support criterion controls denominators of the rational inverse.','proved there','E','For ℓ≠char k, the kernel of the Tate-module cokernel is the ℓ-primary part of the finite étale kernel, so integral invertibility is equivalent toℓ∤deg u. Milne I §7 and Lem10.16 give the divisibility statement. This prime-support assertion survives the correction of the minimal denominator in C-SA06-265.','Classical Tate-module theory.',['MilneAV2008'],['isogeny_denominator'])
add(267,[290],[65],'K14-05:98–110','hom algébrique inverse; indépendante de l','The Lefschetz isomorphism H¹(X)→H^{2n−1}(X)(n−1) induced by an ample class has a rational algebraic divisor/one-cycle inverse, obtained through the Picard/Albanese isogeny associated with the polarization. It is independent of ℓ as a correspondence class.','proof sketched','B','The degree1 Lefschetz standard conjecture is established by Picard/Albanese theory; Kleiman1968 §2A and the abelian-variety cases in§3 give the precise cohomological setting. This rational algebraicity does not give an integral inverse.','Classical polarization theory; published standard-conjecture account1968.',['Kleiman1968','MilneAV2008'])
add(268,[290],[66,67,68],'K14-05:119–167','B_X→B_C→A_C→A_X; polarisante','For a smooth ample complete-intersection curve C in smooth projective X, the composite Pic⁰(X)→Pic⁰(C)→Alb(C)→Alb(X), using the Jacobian polarization, is an isogeny. The restriction has finite kernel and its dual Albanese map is surjective.','proof sketched','E','Milne III §10 proves the relevant abelian-variety quotient-of-Jacobian construction, and I §§8–13 supplies polarization and dual exactness. The construction depends on a smooth complete-intersection curve, available after a suitable ample power over the indicated infinite/algebraically closed field.','Classical Picard/Albanese Lefschetz theory.',['MilneAV2008','Kleiman1968'])
add(269,[291],[68,69,70],'K14-05:169–211','inverse pour H¹ ... cycle de dimension1','A divisor correspondence that induces a rational H¹ isomorphism between the indicated smooth projective varieties has an inverse action represented by a rational one-cycle, by factoring through their Picard/Albanese varieties and polarizations.','proof sketched','B','This is the classical rational degree1 motivic splitting; Voisin2025 §2 Lem2.1 explicitly constructs rational universal zero-cycles through a Jacobian isogeny splitting, while Kleiman1968 gives rational degree1 Lefschetz inverse correspondences. Neither proves integrality.','Classical rational construction; exact modern exposition2025.',['Kleiman1968','MilneAV2008','Voisin2025Universal0'])
add(270,[292],[71],'K14-05:215','cycle entier ... inverse pour H¹(A_X)→H¹(X)','For smooth projective X, ask whether there is an integral codimension-dimX cycle on Alb(X)×X whose action gives the inverse to Albanese pullback on H¹. A rational point of the generic fibre of Sym^n(X)→Alb(X), for some n, is proposed as a sufficient construction.','open on pages','B','The universal assertion is false. Voisin2025 Def1.1 and§2 identify this inverse H¹ Künneth component with an integral universal zero-cycle; Thm1.4 gives a smooth projective complex threefold with representable CH0 but no such cycle. Faithfulness of H¹ on abelian-variety homomorphisms proves the equivalence. A generic symmetric-power section would produce a universal cycle after closure, so the proposed sufficient condition cannot always hold. Rational universal cycles do exist by Lem2.1.','arXiv first2025-08-04; revised2025-10-15.',['Voisin2025Universal0','MilneAV2008'])
add(271,[292],[71],'K14-05:217','cycle entier ... inverse pour H^{2n−1}(X)(n−1)→H¹(B_X)','Ask whether the Picard/top-cohomology isomorphism always has an integral inverse cycle. Already for complex abelian varieties this requires an integral one-cycle on A×A∨ with the inverse Poincaré mixed Künneth component.','open on pages','B','The universal assertion is false by a checked inference from primary results. Subtracting the two factor pushforwards times the zero sections removes the unmixed components of any proposed one-cycle integrally. The remaining component is ±c1(P)^{2g−1}/(2g−1)!, as the exterior-algebra calculation verifies. Beckmann–de Gaay Fortman2023 Thm1.1 then implies integral Hodge for one-cycles on A. Engel–de Gaay Fortman–Schreieder2025/2026 Thm1.1 disproves this for very general principally polarized complex A of dimension≥4. All integral cohomology groups of A are torsion-free.','Equivalence tool first2022-02-10/published2023; counterexample first2025-07-21/revised2026-03-27.',['BeckmannDeGaayFortman2023','EGFS2025Matroids','MilneAV2008'],['poincare_inverse_exterior'])
add(272,[293],[73],'K14-05:222–238','H¹(X,μ_N)≃Pic(X)[N]; 0→Pic⁰[N]→...→NS[N]→0','For proper X over a separably closed field and N prime to the characteristic, the Kummer sequence gives H¹(X,μ_N)≅Pic(X)[N] when global units are N-divisible; for connected geometrically reduced proper X they are the base-field units. Multiplication byN on Pic⁰ is surjective, giving the stated torsion exact sequence to NS[N].','proved there','E','Kummer theory proves the first assertion and Picard divisibility proves the second. Milne1982 §1,p273 records the Picard/NS torsion comparison; prime-to-characteristic hypotheses are indispensable. For nonreduced proper X, prime-to-characteristic powers remain surjective on nilpotent units, so the same conclusion persists under the standard proper finite-type setup.','Classical Kummer sequence; Milne1982 exposition.',['Milne1982Roitman','Stacks03PK'])
add(273,[293,295],[73,74],'K14-05:234–247; K14-06:4–39','H¹(X,Z_l(1))≃T_lPic⁰; φ_X*','The finite NS torsion contributes no Tate module, so inverse limit yields H¹(X,Zℓ(1))≅TℓPic⁰. When Pic⁰_red is an abelian variety dual to Alb(X), Albanese pullback identifies H¹(AlbX,Zℓ) with H¹(X,Zℓ).','proof sketched','E','Milne1982 §1 and Milne Abelian Varieties I Thm12.1 give the Tate-module and duality identifications. The proper normal case in the manuscript is a sufficient situation for the abelian Picard part. Nonreduced Picard structure and p-primary phenomena are not seen by ℓ≠p realization.','Classical Picard theory; sources1982/2008.',['Milne1982Roitman','MilneAV2008'])
add(274,[294,295],[73,74],'K14-05:245–257; K14-06:4–39','0→NS(X)[l]^∨→H^{2n−1}(X,Z_l)(n−1)→H¹(B_X,Z_l)→0','For smooth proper X of pure dimension n over a separably closed field, duality applied to finite-level Kummer sequences gives a finite torsion kernel in H^{2n−1}(X,Zℓ)(n−1) mapping to H¹(Pic⁰_redX,Zℓ). The kernel is dual to the full ℓ-primary NS torsion with the appropriate finite-level twists.','proof sketched','E','Milne1982 §1 makes the finite Picard/NS terms explicit. The notation NS[l] on the sheet is qualified by the margin comp.l-primaire, so interpreting it as only ℓ-torsion would be incorrect. Poincaré duality with finite coefficients and inverse-limit transition maps must be used; simply dualizing free Tate modules loses the finite kernel.','Classical duality; source1982.',['Milne1982Roitman','MilneAV2008'])
add(275,[295],[74],'K14-06:4–39','π1(X)^ab; A_X; 0?','The abelianized étale fundamental-group comparison is controlled by H¹ with finite coefficients and the Albanese prime-to-characteristic Tate module, with the dual NS-torsion contribution and queried exactness endpoints.','open on pages','C','Kummer and Picard duality give the prime-to-characteristic comparison. They do not justify an unqualified isomorphism of the full profinite abelianized group in positive characteristic, nor removal of the finite NS term. Milne1982 §1 is the checked precise comparison; the manuscript endpoints are retained as questions.','Classical prime-to-characteristic theory; assessed2026.',['Milne1982Roitman','MilneAV2008'],confidence='medium')
add(276,[297],[76,78],'K14-06:75–124,181–204','foncteurs fidèles, additifs','The category of abelian varieties has abelian-group Hom sets and biproducts; Tate realization is covariant additive and faithful, and H¹ is contravariant additive and faithful.','proved there','E','Milne I Thm10.15 proves Hom⊗Zℓ injects into homomorphisms of Tate modules and has torsion-free cokernel; it also gives finite freeness of Hom. Surjectivity onto Galois-equivariant linear maps is a separate arithmetic Tate/Faltings theorem and is not asserted here.','Classical; sourceMilne2008.',['MilneAV2008'])
add(277,[297],[78],'K14-06:192–204','H*(A)=Λ*H¹(A)','For an abelian variety A of dimensiong over a separably closed field, ℓ≠char k, cup product gives a canonical isomorphism of graded Zℓ-algebras Λ*H¹(A,Zℓ)≅H*(A,Zℓ); the top exterior power is Zℓ(−g) by the trace.','stated without proof','E','Milne I Thm12.1,p55 gives the exact integral exterior-algebra statement. This controls all exterior-degree and determinant computations in the subsequent pages.','Classical étale cohomology of abelian varieties.',['MilneAV2008'])
add(278,[299],[80,83],'K14-06:289–325,395–427','u_*; dimA=n; dimA′=n′','For u:A→A′ between smooth proper abelian varieties of dimensions n,n′, pushforward sends H^i(A) to H^{i+2(n′−n)}(A′)(n′−n) and is the trace-dual of pullback in complementary degree. For an isogeny, u_*u*=deg(u).','proved there','E','Poincaré duality and the projection formula prove the degree shift and trace identity. Milne I Thm10.9 relates deg(u) to the determinant on a Tate module, and Thm12.1 extends to exterior powers. A changed source/target header in the manuscript cannot be carried through unchanged.','Classical; independent type check2026.',['MilneAV2008'],['exterior_pushforward'])
add(279,[299,300],[81],'K14-06:333–350','u_*1=d[C]; image','If u:A→A′ is generically finite of degree d onto its image abelian subvariety C and dimC=dimA, then u_*[A]=d[C]; if dimC<dimA, the pushforward fundamental cycle is0. The nonzero decomposable wedge determines the rational image subspace through its wedge annihilator.','proved there','E','The first statement is the definition of proper cycle pushforward. The linear-algebra identity W={v:v∧ω=0} for a nonzero top wedgeω∈Λ^rW reconstructs W over a field; Milne Algebraic Groups Lem4.28 gives this exact identity. It determines a rational subspace, not an integral lattice.','Classical pushforward/exterior algebra.',['Fulton1998','MilneAlgebraicGroups2017'],['top_wedge_lattice_counterexample'])
add(280,[300],[83,85],'K14-06:395–427,478–487','déterminer l’image ... degré','A top exterior-power image with its determinant content does not determine the integral image submodule; one ℓ-adic determinant valuation determines only the ℓ-primary contribution to the degree.','false as stated','E','The sublatticesℓZℓe1+Zℓe2 andZℓe1+ℓZℓe2 are different but have the same determinant lineℓZℓ(e1∧e2). They also have the same rational span. Thus the page’s rational recovery of the image subspace is valid, while the stronger integral reading is not.','Elementary lattice counterexample; checked2026.',['MilneAlgebraicGroups2017','MilneAV2008'],['top_wedge_lattice_counterexample'])
add(281,[299],[83],'K14-06:395–427','Λ^{2n−1}u','In cohomological degree i, the transpose describing u_* uses the complementary exterior powerΛ^{2n−i}, with dimensions and twists adjusted to the source; the fixed exponent2n−1 is valid only for i=1.','false as stated','E','The pairingΛ^iV×Λ^{2n−i}V→Λ^{2n}V fixes the exponent. The manuscript’s possible1/i reading is retained for scan review; no general-degree formula with2n−1 can be correct.','Elementary exterior duality, checked2026.',['MilneAV2008'],['exterior_pushforward'])
add(282,[298,299],[76,78,80],'K14-06:75–124,204–234,289–325','projection; produit de Pontryagin; vérifier s’il n’y a pas un signe','Cycle classes respect pullback, cup product, proper pushforward and the Pontryagin product induced by addition on an abelian variety. Duality/transposition introduces graded signs, so identifying two trace pairings requires a chosen exterior-order convention.','proof sketched','E','The functorialities follow from the cycle-class axioms and projection formula (Kleiman1968 §1), while the exterior realization is Milne Thm12.1. The page explicitly requests a sign check; the coordinate calculation in this run fixes a convention but does not decide the ink or establish that the manuscript uses that convention.','Classical formalism; sign audit2026.',['Kleiman1968','MilneAV2008','BeckmannDeGaayFortman2023'],['poincare_inverse_exterior'])
add(283,[297],[76],'K14-06:80–92','C^n(A)=Z','For a connected smooth projective variety over an algebraically closed field, degree identifies zero-cycles modulo algebraic equivalence with Z; in particular this holds for an abelian variety. It is false with rational equivalence in place of algebraic equivalence in positive dimension in general.','stated without proof','E','Points vary in connected algebraic families, so their differences are algebraically trivial; degree is invariant in such families. Mumford1969 shows why the same assertion for rational equivalence is false on abelian surfaces. The manuscript alternative Chow or cycles mod alg must be resolved before applying the displayed C^n=Z.','Classical algebraic equivalence; counterpoint1969.',['Kleiman1968','Mumford1969'])

